WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.875
C.F.N.I.S. and military justice failures.

00:00:02.875 --> 00:00:11.340
The Canadian Forces National Investigation Service investigates crimes within the military — but reports through the same chain of command it must investigate.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.977
The result: zero senior officer convictions through military justice.

00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:21.374
The Military Police Complaints Commission provides civilian oversight but has limited enforcement power.

00:00:21.375 --> 00:00:24.852
The Deschamps Report found a hostile, sexualized culture.

00:00:24.852 --> 00:00:30.329
Seven years later, the Arbour Report made forty-eight more recommendations for structural reform.

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:32.079
The military justice chain.

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:39.909
C.F.N.I.S. investigators report through the chain of command, creating an inherent conflict when investigating senior officers who sit above them.

00:00:39.909 --> 00:00:44.920
The Military Police Complaints Commission has oversight authority but limited enforcement power.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.818
The Judge Advocate General oversees the system.

00:00:47.818 --> 00:00:52.284
This structure makes it structurally impossible to hold the chain of command accountable.

00:00:52.284 --> 00:00:54.522
Systemic investigation failures.

00:00:54.522 --> 00:01:07.204
Documented patterns include: investigations closed without explanation, witnesses discouraged from providing testimony, evidence mishandled or lost, and complainants facing retaliatory career consequences.

00:01:07.204 --> 00:01:11.318
The system consistently protects senior leadership while punishing those who report.

00:01:11.318 --> 00:01:13.227
Independent review findings.

00:01:13.227 --> 00:01:19.204
The Deschamps Report in twenty fifteen found a hostile, sexualized culture throughout the Canadian Armed Forces.

00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:22.193
Seven years of delayed implementation followed.

00:01:22.193 --> 00:01:30.999
The Arbour Report in twenty twenty-two made forty-eight recommendations for structural reform including transferring sexual misconduct cases to civilian jurisdiction.

00:01:30.999 --> 00:01:39.659
The pattern: external reviews document failures, recommendations are made, implementation is delayed or ignored, and another review is commissioned.
